Round-robin TCG (icount) gives each vCPU at most a quarter of the time to the next virtual timer deadline, and the
round-robin kick timer bounds that at 100 ms. A guest thread on one vCPU (a lock holder, a work loop an interrupt woke)
could then sit for up to 75 ms of virtual time while the other three run, longer than a driver's interrupt deadline
(TegraI2C: the transfer's budget plus 50 ms). With a 1 ms kick no vCPU waits more than about 0.75 ms.

